body:
## Intent

Build firstmate's harness-aware supervision framework and wake mechanisms for the verified primary harnesses. Move Claude-shaped watcher instructions out of AGENTS.md into emitted per-harness snippets, keep AGENTS.md harness-neutral, emit exactly one detected harness block at session start, centralize guard and turn-end repair text, add Codex foreground checkpoint support, add Pi's generated primary watcher extension and Pi secondmate launch wiring, add OpenCode's TUI primary watcher plugin, and use the Grok scout result that Grok is Claude-shaped background-notify with its passive Stop-hook only as a backstop. Preserve Claude behavior, avoid contradictory harness instructions, keep fm-spawn changes localized, add focused renderer/session-start/checkpoint/Pi/OpenCode/Grok tests, and ship this firstmate shared tracked-material change through the no-mistakes gate.

## What Changed
- Centralizes firstmate supervision instructions in a harness-aware renderer and session-start output, replacing always-loaded Claude-shaped guidance with one detected primary harness protocol for Claude, Codex, Pi, OpenCode, Grok, and unknown harnesses.
- Adds harness-specific watcher wake paths: Codex foreground checkpoint support, a generated Pi primary watcher extension with secondmate launch wiring, and an OpenCode primary watcher plugin, while preserving Claude-style background notify behavior and Grok's passive backstop shape.
- Hardens watcher ownership, lock, X-mode cadence, turn-end repair, and Pi/OpenCode recovery behavior, with focused tests covering session start rendering, checkpoints, Pi extension generation, watcher locks, turn-end guards, X-mode, and backend edge cases.

## Risk Assessment

⚠️ Medium: Captain, no material defects were found, but residual risk is medium because this is a broad cross-harness supervision change touching wake ownership, turn-end hooks, and generated Pi/OpenCode adapters.

## Testing

The pre-run baseline was already green; on the target commit I ran focused harness/session watcher tests, captured end-user transcript evidence for session start, renderer isolation, Codex checkpointing, and Pi extension generation, then ran the full configured test loop with tmux 3.6a. Everything exited 0, with only the suite's built-in cmux smoke skip for an unavailable socket/auth, and the working tree was clean afterward.

